ECL 26.5.5 library transition
===========================

The proposed package set is:

  ecl         26.5.5+dfsg-1
  maxima-sage 5.50.0-3
  sagemath    10.9-4

Debian's maxima-sage and python3-sage binaries link to libecl. Both must
be rebuilt for libecl26.5. The old and new runtime libraries can coexist,
but the shared /usr/lib/ecl/maxima.fas pathname must contain a module
matching the ECL library used by its loader. Loading the newly compiled
Maxima module with the old Sage ECL extension was reproduced as a memory
access error followed by a segmentation fault.

To prevent a partial upgrade from mixing these modules:

  * ecl Breaks maxima-sage versions before 5.50.0-3~.
  * maxima-sage requires ecl >= 26.5.5+dfsg-1~ and Breaks python3-sage
    versions before 10.9-4~.
  * sagemath requires the updated ECL and maxima-sage packages at build
    time and runtime; its ECL development dependency is also versioned.

Coordinate these source versions when preparing the transition. If a
maintainer chooses different Debian revisions, update the corresponding
versioned relationships in all three source packages together. The
changelogs remain UNRELEASED pending maintainer adoption and upload.

Side-module installation
------------------------
The ecl package now creates and owns /usr/lib/ecl, the directory compiled
into the side-module provider. Other binary packages can install a module
as /usr/lib/ecl/name.fas with dh_install; callers use (require :name).
The module filename is tried with the supplied case and in lowercase.
Consumers no longer need to pass an explicit pathname to REQUIRE.

ECL_SIDE_MODULES_PATH at runtime is a colon-separated list of additional
directories, searched before the build-time defaults. This supports user
or alternative-prefix installations without changing consumer code.
ECL's own core modules retain their existing versioned multiarch layout.
Modules still need to be compiled against a compatible ECL ABI; the new
search provider does not change the coordinated upgrade requirements above.

The unstable amd64 and arm64 Contents indexes list maxima-sage as the
only package shipping an ECL .fas module outside libecl itself. Other
.fas files belong to CLISP or are biological sequence data. Lisp source
packages that compile user-local caches do not need installation changes.

Review branches:

https://github.com/dimpase/ecl-debian/tree/update/ecl-26.5.5
https://github.com/dimpase/maxima-sage-debian/tree/update/ecl-26.5.5
https://github.com/dimpase/sagemath-debian/tree/update/ecl-26.5.5

The archive source dependency check also found SBCL using ECL as its
bootstrap compiler on sh4 only. Its binaries do not need a libecl ABI
transition. The Common Lisp source packages that test ECL, and packages
accepting the virtual lisp-compiler dependency, do not ship modules
linked to libecl that need an archive rebuild.

Validation was performed in Debian unstable arm64, restricted to four
CPUs. Maxima's complete binary package was built; Sage's ECL extension
was rebuilt in the installed Sage 10.9 package, and all 204 ECL and 227
maxima_lib doctests and focused embedded-Maxima checks passed. Default
module discovery and a runtime ECL_SIDE_MODULES_PATH override work in
Sage with MAXIMA_FAS empty. A full Sage package rebuild and the sh4 SBCL
bootstrap have not been performed here.

All 33 archive packages with ECL-specific autopkgtests were checked.
31 pass, including Osicat's 91 checks when run without root privileges.
CFFI's ARM64 char-related failures and cl-plus-ssl's malformed
trivial-sockets system definition occur with the archive ECL too.
Maxima's upstream suite runs 15,551 checks with two failures, in
rtest3 (222) and rtest16 (602); both also fail with the archive build.
